3.	What is the purpose of the sed command? Justify with proper example.
Purpose of sed:
1.	Modify text in files automatically.
2.	Search and replace patterns.
3.	Delete or insert lines.
4.	Extract specific text or lines from a file or output.
 
Basic Syntax:
sed [options] 'command' filename
•	command is usually something like s/pattern/replacement/ for substitution.
•	filename is the file you want to edit.
 
Examples:
1.	Substitute text in a file
sed 's/oldtext/newtext/' example.txt
•	Replaces the first occurrence of oldtext with newtext in each line of example.txt.
•	Example:
Content of example.txt:
Hello World
Hello Linux
Command:
sed 's/Hello/Hi/' example.txt
Output:
Hi World
Hi Linux
 
2.	Delete a specific line
sed '2d' example.txt
•	Deletes line 2 of the file.
 
3.	Print specific lines
sed -n '1,3p' example.txt
•	Prints lines 1 to 3 only (-n suppresses automatic printing).
 
4.	Replace all occurrences in each line
sed 's/oldtext/newtext/g' example.txt
•	g stands for global, replaces all occurrences in a line, not just the first.


4.	Which distinct command is used to display one-line descriptions of any commands?
The command used to display one-line descriptions of any command in Linux is:
whatis
 
Explanation:
•	whatis shows a brief, one-line description of a command.
•	It searches the manual page database (man pages) for the specified command.
•	Useful when you want a quick summary without opening the full manual.
 
Syntax:
whatis command_name
 
Example:
whatis ls
Output:
ls (1)               - list directory contents
•	Here, (1) indicates the section of the man pages (general commands).
•	The description list directory contents is the one-line summary.
 
✅ Conclusion:
whatis is ideal for quickly understanding what a command does without reading the full manual.
For example:
whatis grep
Output:
grep (1)              - print lines that match patterns
It’s simpler than man if you just want a short description.

