7.	Write the command to give read, write, and execute permission to the owner of a file script.sh.
To give read, write, and execute permissions to the owner of a file script.sh, you can use the chmod command:
chmod u+rwx script.sh
 
Explanation:
•	chmod → change mode, used to modify file permissions.
•	u → user (the owner of the file).
•	rwx → read (r), write (w), execute (x) permissions.
•	script.sh → the file you are modifying.
 
Example:
ls -l script.sh
Before:
-rw-r--r-- 1 user user 1024 Oct 13 19:40 script.sh
chmod u+rwx script.sh
ls -l script.sh
After:
-rwxr--r-- 1 user user 1024 Oct 13 19:40 script.sh
•	The owner now has full permissions (rwx).
•	Group and others remain unchanged.

